every Friday we are taking time to  answer your most pressing questions  about the coronavirus and this morning  with many states partially lifting  restrictions we're talking about how to  safely venture out in public big  question there this is despite the fact  that a recent poll found 67 percent of  Americans would be uncomfortable  visiting a clothing store and 78 percent  say they'd be uncomfortable eating in a  restaurant CBS News senior medical  correspondent dr. Tara Narula joins us  now to walk us through some of the  guidance the latest guidance anyhow so  as people go outside whether they're  going to a restaurant or a clothing  store or just taking a walk what  precautions should they take to keep  themselves safe well Tony it's true  we're all beginning to emerge from our  cocoon  but life as we know it is definitely  going to be different and it's important  that we take these precautions really  until we have herd immunity or we have a  vaccine so first and foremost it's going  to be important to try to keep that six  feet distance in mind when you're going  out and about and really minimize your  time and those crowded maybe poorly  ventilated spaces wearing a homemade or  cloth mask is still going to be helpful  given the rates of what we know are  asymptomatic trance transmission in  addition you want to practice all those  good hygiene things we've talked about  hand-washing sanitizer disinfecting  surfaces using your elbow to touch the  elevator door button the elevator button  and then you want to try to keep as  little contact as possible so online  banking telemedicine having your  pharmacy prescriptions delivered to your  home food delivery and even things like  Apple pay so you don't have to actually  exchange cash or get your credit card  out and then finally I know we're all  gonna be tempted to hug and kiss and  really get close to each other but you  are gonna want to continue the elbow  bump or the foot tap and and really  trying to keep your distance all right  we've got a viewer question from Laura  who is interested in knowing whether if  she and a friend are both wearing masks  and they're outside can they get within  six feet of one another for somewhat  normal interaction well that's a great  question and really the mask is still  going to be important in the sense that  again we know there's this asymptomatic  transmission of the virus  the mask is not airtight especially the  ones that people are wearing at home the  non n95 masks and so viruses can  essentially go around the mask or  through the mask or land on the mask and  so you can get infected and really what  they're meant for is preventing you from  infecting others with those large  droplets but it's really not a license  to break that six feet rule just because  you're both wearing masks all right we  also have a question here from Julie Ann  who emailed in to ask a question I have  as well is it safe to take your kids to  the park and then I would just add on to  that if they see other kids in the park  can you let them do what kids do get  together and have fun well we have two  kids and we've been taking them to the  park every single day you know kids need  that fresh air and exercise they should  be getting 60 minutes of physical  activity every day and the park is a  great place for that because usually it  is a large wide space where kids can  maintain distance from others so  important things to remember if you do  bring toys or things for your kids are  gonna wash them when you come home and  we were in the situation recently where  another family sort of wandered over and  started playing with the balls that our  kids were playing with and we really had  to say you know listen I think we really  need to keep a little bit of distance  right now so I don't think there's  anything wrong with being vocal about  that if that happens and and again you  want to avoid things like playgrounds  where there's a lot of touching of the  monkey bars and the swings so open wide  space in the park is the best idea away  from others all right and we've got a  related question actually from a viewer  named Amy related because so many  families including mine we are  sheltering in place but other families  are as well they have kids we have kids  can those two families that are both  sheltering get together safely for the  sake of the kids if nothing else well  it's it's understandable that people  want to get together and the issue is  really that you don't know where the  other family has been what they've been  touching who they've been interacting  with so just because you've been  sheltering in place we're not living in  a bubble and it doesn't mean that you're  not going to be exposed to the virus and  potentially be able to transmit the  virus so it is a good idea  to continue that separation as hard as  it  in fact even from New York we have the  most recent numbers showing that of the  last several days most of the  hospitalizations were coming from people  who were at home who were not travelling  and not working so that just goes to  tell you that shelter in place does not  mean you are immune from getting the  virus yeah that's a really good point  dr. taryn Narula thank you a great  guidance as always
 

